# Search & Sorting — Notes

## Binary Search — the real principle

Binary search is NOT "search a sorted array." It's:

> **At each step, can I discard half the array with certainty?**

If yes → binary search applies, even if the array isn't fully sorted.
Sorted order is just ONE way to get that certainty. Rotation, slopes, and
monotone properties are others.

### How to recognize it
1. Problem asks for **O(log n)** or "better than O(n)" → strong signal.
2. You need **ONE point** with a property, not all of them.
3. You can decide which half to keep by looking at **mid** (and maybe a neighbor
   or an endpoint).
4. The array has **some structure** (sorted, rotated, slope, monotone).

If 1 and 3 hold, it's almost certainly binary search.

### Two flavors
```
Classic (sorted array):   search a VALUE → compare mid with the target
Pivot / Peak (not fully sorted): search a POINT → compare mid with a NEIGHBOR
                                  or an ENDPOINT to read the local shape
```

### Overflow-safe mid
```cpp
int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;   // not (left + right) / 2
```
`left + right` can overflow when values are near INT_MAX. Always use this form.

---

## Find Peak Element (slope intuition)

Picture the array as a mountain/wave. From the middle, look at the slope:
```
nums[mid] > nums[mid+1]  → going DOWN → a peak is at mid or to the LEFT  → right = mid
nums[mid] < nums[mid+1]  → going UP   → the climb continues RIGHT        → left  = mid + 1
```
**Why it always works:** keep climbing uphill. Either you keep rising until the
end (the border is -∞, so the last element is a peak), or you start falling (the
point just before the fall is a peak). A peak is always guaranteed in the uphill
direction — the other values don't matter.

The asymmetry (`mid+1` going up, `mid` going down):
- Going up, `mid` can't be the peak (its right neighbor is bigger) → discard it.
- Going down, `mid` might be the peak (right neighbor is smaller) → keep it.

```cpp
int left = 0, right = nums.size() - 1;
while (left < right) {
    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
    if (nums[mid] > nums[mid + 1]) right = mid;     // going down
    else left = mid + 1;                            // going up
}
return left;
```
O(log n) always — no bad worst case (each step halves the range).

---

## Find Pivot / Rotated Array (same family)

Same idea as peak: binary search on a not-fully-sorted array, deciding the side
by comparing mid with a reference point.
```
Find Min in Rotated:  compare nums[mid] with nums[right] → which half is sorted
Search in Rotated:    find which half is sorted, then check if target is in it
```
The shared question: "which half can I discard with certainty?"

### Family to drill
```
Find Peak Element (162)                slope
Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted (153)   rotation
Search in Rotated Sorted Array (33)    rotation + value search
First Bad Version (278)                monotone (all-bad after a point)
Search a 2D Matrix (74)                2D order
```

