## Sorting small/fixed groups

### Counting sort (k known small groups)
Count each value, then rewrite the array in order. Works for ANY number of
groups, as long as values are small integers usable as indices.
```cpp
vector<int> freq(k, 0);
for (int x : nums) freq[x]++;
int idx = 0;
for (int v = 0; v < k; v++)
    while (freq[v]-- > 0) nums[idx++] = v;
```
O(n) time, O(k) space. Two passes (count + rebuild).

### Dutch National Flag (exactly 3 groups, one pass)
Three pointers partition into low / mid / high regions in a single pass.
```cpp
int low = 0, mid = 0, high = nums.size() - 1;
while (mid <= high) {
    if (nums[mid] == 0) { swap(nums[low], nums[mid]); low++; mid++; }
    else if (nums[mid] == 1) { mid++; }
    else { swap(nums[mid], nums[high]); high--; }   // mid does NOT advance
}
```
The one tricky detail: on a swap with `high`, **mid does not advance** — the
value you just pulled from `high` hasn't been examined yet. On a swap with
`low`, mid DOES advance (the value from low is already known to be a 1).

```
3 groups, in-place, one pass, O(1) space  → Dutch flag
k groups, values in small range           → counting sort
```

