## Frontier pointer (partition / move elements)

A slow pointer marks where the next "chosen" element goes; a fast pointer scans.
Swap and advance the frontier only when the condition holds.
```cpp
int idx = 0;                       // frontier: next slot for a chosen element
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (nums[i] is chosen) {
        swap(nums[idx], nums[i]);
        idx++;                     // frontier advances only on placement
    }
}
```
**Hook:** it's the slow/fast pattern from linked lists, but in an array with swaps.
**Family:** Move Zeroes, Remove Element, Remove Duplicates, Sort Colors, quicksort partition.
**Signal:** "in-place" + "move/separate/group elements by a condition".

