## Selection without full sort

### Kth Largest — three approaches
```
Max-heap with all elements:  O(n log n) time, O(n) space   — push all, pop k-1
Min-heap of size K:          O(n log k) time, O(k) space    — keep only K largest
Quickselect:                 O(n) average, O(1) extra       — O(n²) worst case
```

**Min-heap of size K** (the clean "top-K" pattern):
```cpp
priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> minHeap;
for (int num : nums) {
    minHeap.push(num);
    if (minHeap.size() > k) minHeap.pop();   // drop the smallest
}
return minHeap.top();   // smallest of the K largest = Kth largest
```
Each op is O(log k) because the heap never exceeds K — that's the whole point.

**Quickselect** — O(n) average:
Pick a pivot, partition, recurse into ONLY the side that holds the answer.
```
average: n + n/2 + n/4 + ... = O(n)   (recurse one side, halving)
worst:   n + (n-1) + ... + 1 = O(n²)  (bad pivots, e.g. sorted input)
```
Mitigate the worst case with a random pivot. Mention "O(n) average, O(n²) worst"
in interviews.

---

## Quick complexity reference
```
Binary search (any flavor):  O(log n) time, O(1) space iterative
Counting sort:               O(n + k) time, O(k) space
Dutch flag:                  O(n) time, O(1) space
Min-heap top-K:              O(n log k) time, O(k) space
Quickselect:                 O(n) average / O(n²) worst, O(1) extra
Comparison sort (std::sort): O(n log n) time
```
